6, 1, 6, 5, 2, 3, 7, 2, 7, 6, 3, 0, 4, 3, 4, 7]}, "m": "palebrick"}, {"mesh": {"v": [41.26, -5.61, 0, 51.76, 0.21, 0, 37.21, 26.45, 0, 26.72, 20.63, 0, 41.26, -5.61, 14, 51.76, 0.21, 14, 37.21, 26.45, 14, 26.72, 20.63, 14], "f": [0, 2, 1, 0, 3, 2, 4, 5, 6, 4, 6, 7, 0, 1, 5, 0, 5, 4, 1, 2, 6, 1, 6, 5, 2, 3, 7, 2, 7, 6, 3, 0, 4, 3, 4, 7]}, "m": "concrete"}, {"mesh": {"v": [-8.6, -43.54, 0, 45.62, -13.48, 0, 41.26, -5.61, 0, -12.97, -35.66, 0, -8.6, -43.54, 6, 45.62, -13.48, 6, 41.26, -5.61, 6, -12.97, -35.66, 6], "f": [0, 2, 1, 0, 3, 2, 4, 5, 6, 4, 6, 7, 0, 1, 5, 0, 5, 4, 1, 2, 6, 1, 6, 5, 2, 3, 7, 2, 7, 6, 3, 0, 4, 3, 4, 7]}, "m": "palebrick"}], "notes": "Dia Chelsea, West 22nd Street (renovated by Architecture Research Office, 2021): three former industrial buildings side by side, brick, one and two storeys, top-lit galleries under skylights, tall steel-framed windows and the entrance on the street; on the sidewalk, Joseph Beuys's 7000 Oaks trees each paired with a basalt column; taller Chelsea gallery and loft buildings round it. Turned 29 degrees to the Manhattan grid. Written from general knowledge; guessed: the three buildings' heights and widths (about 60 m of frontage, 30 m deep), the window layout, the neighbours. No photographs were available. Refined 6 Oct 2026 from published descriptions and the ground file's OpenStreetMap outlines, not photographs: the whole block, the street and the 7000 Oaks with it, moved 6 m east and 8 m north so that West 22nd Street lies on the street the ground shows (it was running about 10 m south of it) and the buildings stand on the block's outlines (on 81% to 95%). Doubt: the ground gives eight and nine storeys for most of the cells the galleries cover and two storeys only for a narrow strip to the west, while Dia Chelsea is one and two storeys; either the museum's point is off by a lot or the outlines' heights belong to the lofts behind, so the low galleries were kept as published and the position follows the street."}